# Dagger / local verification pipeline

Path-scoped routing only. Full procedure lives in skill `dagger-go-pipeline`.

## When this rule applies

Changes to Dagger functions, check manifests, package manifests/lockfiles,
migrations exercised by composed checks, Keycloak/runtime overlays, Playwright
composed execution, or verification classification.

## Required routing

1. Classify `PIPELINE_IMPACT` (`NONE` | `EXISTING_CHECK_SUFFICIENT` |
   `PIPELINE_CHANGE_REQUIRED`) in the Technical Architect plan.
2. Load `dagger-go-pipeline` whenever impact is not `NONE`.
3. Preserve Phase D **local-only** Dagger scope: no remote CI provider choice,
   GitHub Actions, `act`, deployment/release automation, or Dagger Cloud unless
   a binding repository decision already authorises it.
4. Inspect the current graph before adding a callable. Reuse existing
   functions, service graphs and named tool caches.
5. Prefer the smallest meaningful Directory/File inputs and existing source
   boundaries (`backend`, `frontend`, `dagger`, `governance`).
6. Run focused pipeline verification (`go test ./pure ./cmd/...`, relevant
   `dagger call …`, or `tools/ci/verify-dagger-architecture.sh` when topology
   or cache policy changed).
7. Do not claim named-cache or result-cache reuse without observed evidence.
8. Do not treat a bare repeated `dagger call` as a second runtime proof when
   Dagger result caching may skip execution — use an explicit `proofNonce`
   (or equivalent input) for an independent fresh stack.

## Out of scope here

Do not paste the full cache checklist into this rule. Cache allow/deny lists,
input-boundary rules and Go quality gates belong in `dagger-go-pipeline` and
`docs/ci/DAGGER-IMPLEMENTATION.md`.
